= Unit 1 · Groups and cyclic structure

== Lecture 1 · From operations to groups
#focus[Recognize a group, prove elementary identities, and distinguish a definition from a property. Prerequisites: functions, composition, integers, and proof by contradiction.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall associativity permits reassociation but not reordering.


A symmetry is an action that can be performed and reversed. Rotating a square twice is again a symmetry; doing nothing is a symmetry; reversing a rotation is possible. These three observations suggest associativity, an identity, and inverses. Commutativity is deliberately absent: a reflection followed by a rotation need not equal the reverse order.

#definition[Group][A group is a set $G$ with an operation $G times G -> G$, written $(a,b) |-> a b$, such that $(a b)c=a(b c)$, there is $e$ with $e a=a e=a$, and every $a$ has $a^(-1)$ with $a a^(-1)=a^(-1)a=e$. It is *abelian* if $a b=b a$ for all $a,b$.]

The operation is part of the structure. The integers form a group under addition, but not multiplication: $2$ has no integer multiplicative inverse. Nonzero rational numbers form a multiplicative group. In $S_X$, the bijections of a set $X$, the operation is composition. Composition is associative because both $(f g)h$ and $f(g h)$ send $x$ to $f(g(h(x)))$. The inverse of a bijection provides the inverse required by the group axioms.

#theorem[Identity, inverses, and cancellation][A group has a unique identity and a unique inverse for each element. If $a b=a c$, then $b=c$. Also $(a b)^(-1)=b^(-1)a^(-1)$.]
#strategy[Uniqueness proofs compare two candidates. Cancellation uses multiplication by an inverse, with parentheses justified by associativity.]
#proof[If $e,e'$ are identities, then $e=e e'=e'$. If $b,c$ are inverses of $a$, then $b=b(a c)=(b a)c=c$. From $a b=a c$, multiply on the left by $a^(-1)$ and reassociate to obtain $b=c$. Finally $(a b)(b^(-1)a^(-1))=a(b b^(-1))a^(-1)=e$, and the reverse product is also $e$. Uniqueness identifies this as the inverse.]

#example[Solving without commuting][In $G$, the equation $a x b=c$ has the unique solution $x=a^(-1)c b^(-1)$. Multiplying on the correct side matters. The expression $c a^(-1)b^(-1)$ need not work. Substitution verifies existence; cancellation proves uniqueness.]

For an integer $n$, define $a^0=e$, positive powers by repeated multiplication, and $a^(-n)=(a^(-1))^n$. Induction gives $a^(m+n)=a^m a^n$ for integers $m,n$. This law concerns powers of one element; it does not justify $(a b)^2=a^2 b^2$ in an arbitrary group.

#warning[Closure is a condition on the chosen set. Nonzero matrices are not a group under multiplication, since a nonzero matrix can be singular. The invertible matrices $"GL"_n(K)$ are a group.]
#example[A calculation that detects commutativity][Suppose $(a b)^2=a^2 b^2$ for all $a,b$ in a group. Expanding gives $a b a b=a a b b$. Multiply on the left by $a^(-1)$ and on the right by $b^(-1)$ to get $b a=a b$. Thus this familiar-looking square rule, if universally valid, actually forces the group to be abelian. The converse follows by commuting the middle factors. This is a useful test of an algebraic shortcut: ask what additional property it would imply if it held for every pair.]

To present a group example well, name the set and operation, then verify closure before inverses. Writing a symbolic inverse is insufficient if it lies outside the chosen set.

#checkpoint("1.1", [Why does $a b=e$ imply $b a=e$ in a group?], [Multiplication by $a^(-1)$ shows $b=a^(-1)$; hence $b a=e$.])
#takeaways[Prove structural facts from the axioms before using them as algebraic shortcuts. Study guide U1 revisits cancellation; exercises U1.01–U1.04 test the basic definitions.]
